{"language": "en", "duration": 94.28, "text": "Money doesn't grow on trees. You've probably heard that sentence as a kid, and it may still be steering your choices today. Researchers call these money phrases beliefs or money scripts. Psychologist Brad Klontz writes about them. Ideas learned early, rarely chosen. Think about what was said at your home. We can't afford it. Rich people are dishonest. We don't talk about money at the table. Money can't buy happiness. Each of these phrases was repeated dozens of times. And never once discussed. As an adult, they turn into reflexes. If you grew up hearing we can't afford it, you avoid looking at your account. If you heard money doesn't grow on trees, you feel guilty spending, even when you can't afford it. If you heard rich people are dishonest, you distrust success, including your own. The trap is that you don't see them. They sound like common sense. You never decided to believe them. It's a habit set long before you could judge it. So how do you free yourself? First, spot the phrase. When you hesitate to spend to ask for a raise or to look at your account, ask yourself What sentence do I hear in my head? Then find where it comes from. Who said it and in what situation? Often it was protecting someone from a specific reality that may no longer be yours. Finally rewrite it. Not to deny it, but to make it true today. Money doesn't grow on trees can become money gets built. And I'm allowed to enjoy it. And you, which money phrase did you hear at home? Write it in the comments.", "words": [{"word": "\"Money", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.54}, {"word": "doesn't", "start": 0.54, "end": 1.12}, {"word": "grow", "start": 1.12, "end": 1.32}, {"word": "on", "start": 1.32, "end": 1.54}, {"word": "trees.\"", "start": 1.54, "end": 1.8}, {"word": "You've", "start": 2.34, "end": 2.74}, {"word": "probably", "start": 2.74, "end": 3.18}, {"word": "heard", "start": 3.18, "end": 3.44}, {"word": "that", "start": 3.44, "end": 3.62}, {"word": "sente